          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Diabetes guidance is not in full agreement on the starting age. In the retrieved guidance on who is screened for type 2 diabetes, two authorities differ:\n\n- **Diabetes association guidance** screens all adults from age 35, regardless of weight. It also screens younger adults who are overweight or obese and have at least one additional risk factor, such as a close relative with diabetes, high blood pressure, polycystic ovary syndrome, or a history of gestational diabetes. A lower body-mass threshold applies to people of Asian ancestry.\n- **Preventive-services guidance** limits routine screening to adults aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese. It supports screening in that group because screening trials show benefit there.\n\nThe two positions lead to different numbers of people being tested, so the answer depends on which guidance you follow. The retrieved passages do not say how strong the evidence is behind either age threshold, so I cannot tell you how firmly each position is supported.\n\nSource: \"Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds\" (National diabetes association standards of care and preventive services guidance, educational summary).\n\nA clinician can tell you which screening approach fits your own age, weight and risk factors.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
